WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.962 --> 00:00:04.329
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:12.270
CarTel is a mobile sensor
network data collection system.

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:15.090
The idea is we put a little
bit of hardware in cars--

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:18.300
this hardware consists of a
Linux box and some sensors--

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.120
and then we use this data that
we've collected from the cars

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:24.020
to answer sort of interesting
questions about the world.

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:25.770
So we synthesize data
from a bunch of cars

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:29.940
together in order do
things like give users

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:32.430
a picture of what traffic in
the city currently looks like.

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:35.850
We have a range of sensors
that are deployed in the cars.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:39.300
All of the cars include a GPS,
a Global Positioning System,

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:42.090
as well as a Wi-Fi radio that
they can use for getting data

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:43.290
off of the cars.

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:45.210
Other sensors that we
put on some of the cars

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:48.270
include accelerometers, which
can do things like measure

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:50.843
the quality of the ride,
or the surface of the road

00:00:50.843 --> 00:00:53.010
as the car drives over it,
so that, for example, you

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:56.580
can detect vibrations or
bumps as the car hits them.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.950
In the CarTel project, we
have several different ways

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:00.600
of transmitting
data off the cars.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:01.380
And one of things we've--

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.255
one of the technologies
we've been working on

00:01:03.255 --> 00:01:06.660
is developing different
means of data transmission.

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.180
And the most basic version
of data transmission

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:10.450
is just to use a cell phone.

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:13.490
So this is somebody has
a cellular data plan,

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:15.240
they connect the cell
phone up to the car,

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:19.898
they connect a wireless data
card up to the Linux boxes

00:01:19.898 --> 00:01:22.315
in the car, and then use that
to transmit information off.

00:01:22.315 --> 00:01:23.773
And one of the
things that we spend

00:01:23.773 --> 00:01:25.530
a lot of time developing
in the project

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:28.080
is technology to use Wi-Fi.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:29.760
Part of the technology
we've developed

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:32.940
is to-- allows cars as they
drive by these wireless access

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:35.905
points to very quickly
associate with the access points

00:01:35.905 --> 00:01:37.530
and transmit little
bits of information

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.540
every time they see one
of these access points

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:42.277
that's open and
available for access.

00:01:42.277 --> 00:01:43.860
As the cars are
driving along, they're

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:44.985
transmitting this data off.

00:01:44.985 --> 00:01:47.130
And what happens is
that they transmit it

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:52.493
to a server or a portal that's
running on a machine at MIT.

00:01:52.493 --> 00:01:54.660
And that portal is in charge
of taking all this data

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:56.430
and synthesizing it
together in order

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:59.910
to present a sort of global
view of what's going on

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:02.040
in the world, or what
these sensors in cars

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:03.630
are observing about the world.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:06.810
Typically, this data
gets put into a sort

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:11.039
of a visualization that gets
overlaid on top of a map.

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:12.706
Users can then browse
this visualization

00:02:12.706 --> 00:02:14.581
in order to learn various
things about what's

00:02:14.581 --> 00:02:15.820
going on inside the world.

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:17.250
We have a range of
different applications

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:17.850
that we've developed.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:20.250
So the first application
is-- and the simplest one--

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:23.070
is just one that allows users
to browse their own driving

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:23.707
information.

00:02:23.707 --> 00:02:25.290
So you may be
interested, for example,

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:27.090
in comparing the
amount of time that it

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:30.123
takes for you to drive
from home to work

00:02:30.123 --> 00:02:31.290
on various different routes.

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:33.210
So you can compare
your freeway drives

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:35.730
to your not freeway
drives, if you

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:37.980
have several different routes
from your house to work

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:39.330
or from your work to your house.

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:41.760
Other applications that do
sort of more interesting things

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:43.635
that combine lots of
data from different cars

00:02:43.635 --> 00:02:46.950
together include one that we
call the Pothole Patrol, which

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:50.400
is an application where we use
these accelerometers on the car

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:53.070
that are measuring the quality
of the ride to detect where

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:54.660
potholes are in
the city of Boston,

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:57.620
and then categorize them
in terms of their severity.

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:00.120
The third application that we
spent a lot of time developing

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.860
is a traffic portal.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:03.780
So the idea here is
that we use the cars,

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:05.760
as they're driving along,
we can learn things

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.670
about how fast they were
able to drive on every road.

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:11.370
And then for each road segment,
each little piece of road,

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:14.010
like, for example, Mass
Ave in front of MIT,

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:17.100
we can compute a
distribution of the delays

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:19.680
that your car has experienced
when they were driving on that.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:27.330
The first goal is to explore
the use of cars or mobile nodes

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:28.908
as sensing devices.

00:03:28.908 --> 00:03:30.450
So how can we use
these mobile things

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:31.750
in order to measure the world?

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:32.542
And so it's about--

00:03:32.542 --> 00:03:34.020
one goal is about
just developing

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:38.873
the technology that's needed to
get data off of these cars that

00:03:38.873 --> 00:03:41.040
are moving around in the
world, and to bring it back

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:43.420
to a server, and to synthesize
it and store it together.

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:45.420
And some of the challenges
there include things

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:47.610
like dealing with
variable connectivity,

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:50.610
that the cell phones will cut
in and out as you drive around

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:53.620
or there isn't continuous
wireless conductivity available

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:54.120
everywhere.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.620
So one goal is sort
of a technology goal.

00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:58.270
The other goal is
an application goal,

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:00.560
which is to develop
some of these-- use

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:04.110
these cars in order to
develop applications that

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:06.390
are sort of new and
different, and enable

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:09.120
things that will help people
besides computer scientists.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:10.620
This traffic portal
that I mentioned

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:13.440
is an example of something that
everybody could take advantage

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:14.843
of.

00:04:14.843 --> 00:04:17.010
Anybody would be interested
in knowing what the best

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:18.839
route to take from A to B is.

00:04:18.839 --> 00:04:20.880
Obviously, things like
MapQuest and Google Maps

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:21.930
have huge uptake.

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:24.240
And adding traffic
to those would just

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:25.620
increase their value.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:26.700
So one of the things
that we're doing in order

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:28.380
to bring the cost
of this box down

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:30.360
is we're looking
at solutions that

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:33.213
include smaller amounts of
hardware inside of the car

00:04:33.213 --> 00:04:35.630
because we think that hardware
cost is a barrier to entry.

00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:38.340
So that, we think, will allow us
to produce a piece of hardware

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:41.610
that goes into the cars that
maybe only costs $30 or $40

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:44.335
per car, which would be
something that we think

00:04:44.335 --> 00:04:45.960
an average person
might be willing just

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:49.880
to put in their car in order to
benefit from this technology.

